<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lantingji Xu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">353</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Orchid Pavilion Preface</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lantingji Xu (Preface to the Poems Composed at the Orchid Pavilion) is a short prose text written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-xizhi">Wang Xizhi</a> in 353 for a collection of poems composed by his friends at a spring gathering at the Orchid Pavilion near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a>. Regarded as the most celebrated work of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calligraphy">calligraphy</a>, the original was reportedly buried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizong-of-tang">Emperor Taizong of Tang</a>, and it survives only in copies and tracings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lantingji-xu.jpg" alt="Lantingji Xu" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XingshuLantingxv.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
